## What Problem This Solves
In the TUI, switching sessions twice in quick succession can silently drop you back onto the first session. When a user runs `/session A` then `/session B` before A's history finishes loading, and A's `chat.history` RPC happens to resolve after B's, the stale A completion reverts the active session back to A.

`setSession` sets `state.currentSessionKey` synchronously and then awaits `loadHistory()`, but nothing serializes overlapping switches. The editor submit path is fire-and-forget (`void Promise.resolve(run()).catch(...)` in `src/tui/tui-submit.ts`) and the `session` case in `src/tui/tui-command-handlers.ts` has no reentrancy guard, so two switches can be in flight at once. Inside `loadHistory`, everything after the `await` runs unconditionally against whatever the RPC returned:

```ts
// before: src/tui/tui-session-actions.ts
const loadHistory = async (): Promise<TuiHistoryLoadResult> => {
  try {
    const history = await client.loadHistory({ sessionKey: state.currentSessionKey, ... });
    const sessionInfo = record.sessionInfo;
    if (sessionInfo?.key && sessionInfo.key !== state.currentSessionKey) {
      updateAgentFromSessionKey(sessionInfo.key);
      state.currentSessionKey = sessionInfo.key; // reverts to A after B already applied
      updateHeader();
    }
    state.currentSessionId = ...;               // reverts to A's id
    // chat log is rebuilt from A's messages
    if (inFlightRunId) {
      state.activeChatRunId = inFlightRunId;    // adopts A's abandoned run
    }
  } catch (err) {
    chatLog.addSystem(`history failed: ${String(err)}`); // stale A failure into B's transcript
    tui.requestRender(true);
  }
```

When A's load resolves last, `sessionInfo.key` ("A") no longer equals the now-current key ("B"), so this block reverts `currentSessionKey`, `currentSessionId`, rebuilds the chat log from A's stale messages, and adopts A's in-flight run onto `activeChatRunId`. If A's load instead *rejects* last, the `catch` writes a `history failed:` notice and forces a render against B's transcript. The sibling `refreshSessionInfo()` in the same file already guards overlap (`refreshSessionInfoInFlight` / `refreshSessionInfoQueued`); `setSession` / `loadHistory` never got the equivalent.

## Why This Change Was Made
Give `setSession` / `loadHistory` a monotonic generation token that mirrors the existing `refreshSessionInfo` overlap intent. `setSession` bumps the generation on each switch; `loadHistory` captures it before the RPC and no-ops its completion, on both the success and failure paths, if a newer switch has since advanced the generation:

```ts
// after: src/tui/tui-session-actions.ts
let sessionSwitchGeneration = 0;

const loadHistory = async (): Promise<TuiHistoryLoadResult> => {
  const generation = sessionSwitchGeneration;
  try {
    const history = await client.loadHistory({ sessionKey: state.currentSessionKey, ... });
    if (generation !== sessionSwitchGeneration) {
      return { loaded: false };
    }
    ...
  } catch (err) {
    if (generation !== sessionSwitchGeneration) {
      return { loaded: false };
    }
    chatLog.addSystem(`history failed: ${String(err)}`);
    tui.requestRender(true);
    return { loaded: false };
  }
};

const setSession = async (rawKey: string) => {
  sessionSwitchGeneration += 1;
  ...
  await loadHistory();
};
```

`loadHistory` is the single function that owns the post-RPC mutation of `currentSessionKey` / `currentSessionId` / `activeChatRunId`, the chat-log rebuild, and the failure notice, so guarding it there fixes both clobbers regardless of how overlapping calls originate (fire-and-forget editor submit, the `session` command case, or a `sessions.changed` push); no redundant reentrancy guard is added at each call site. The failure path is guarded symmetrically with the success path: a superseded switch whose history RPC rejects (aborted/failed) no longer leaks a stale `history failed:` line into the session the user has already moved to. The generation only advances on an intentional switch (`setSession`), so same-session reloads keep their existing behavior, and a stale completion returns the existing `{ loaded: false }` shape that callers already handle. This complements the merged #96980, whose generation guard lives in `tui-event-handlers.ts` gating the `sessions.changed` push-reload path; that PR's only change to `tui-session-actions.ts` was plumbing the `TuiHistoryLoadResult` return shape, not a switch-race guard.

## User Impact
After the race, `sendMessage` reads the live `state.currentSessionKey` at call time, so the user's next typed message, believing they are on B, is silently routed to session A instead. This is real session misrouting, not a cosmetic glitch. On the reject path, a `history failed:` notice for the abandoned switch appears in the transcript of the session the user actually landed on. The switch is an ordinary user action (two quick `/session` commands where the first's RPC lands second), not an artificial race. No security boundary is crossed; both sessions belong to the same local user.

## Real behavior proof
Behavior addressed: In the TUI, two quick `/session` switches where the first session's `chat.history` RPC lands last silently revert the active session, so the user's next typed message is routed to the session they already left.
Real environment tested: The real `createSessionActions` production factory from `src/tui/tui-session-actions.ts` driven end to end through its real `setSession` / `loadHistory` closures, on pristine `main` 69554995c1 and on PR head 66c6503daa. The gateway `chat.history` RPC and the chat-log sink are supplied through the same injected seams production constructs them with; the switch sequencing, generation logic, state mutation, and transcript rebuild are all the shipped code.
Exact steps or command run after this patch: `node --import tsx drive.mts` where the driver calls `setSession("agent:main:A")` then `setSession("agent:main:B")` without awaiting, resolves B's history first and the earlier A's history last (carrying key `agent:main:A`, id `session-a`), then reads the resulting session state and transcript rows.
Evidence after fix:
```
# BEFORE (pristine main 69554995c1, identical input)
user runs: /session agent:main:A then /session agent:main:B
gateway chat.history in flight for: agent:main:A, agent:main:B
resolving agent:main:B first, then the earlier agent:main:A last (out of order)

active session key : agent:main:A
active session id  : session-a
active chat run id : null
transcript rows    : ["message from B","message from A"]

next typed message would be routed to: agent:main:A  [MISROUTED, user is on B]

# AFTER (this patch, head 66c6503daa, identical input)
user runs: /session agent:main:A then /session agent:main:B
gateway chat.history in flight for: agent:main:A, agent:main:B
resolving agent:main:B first, then the earlier agent:main:A last (out of order)

active session key : agent:main:B
active session id  : session-b
active chat run id : null
transcript rows    : ["message from B"]

next typed message would be routed to: agent:main:B  [correct, user is on B]
```
Observed result after fix: On identical input, the stale `agent:main:A` completion no longer overwrites the session the user switched to. The active session key stays `agent:main:B`, its id stays `session-b`, A's message is no longer replayed into B's transcript, and the user's next typed message is routed to `agent:main:B` instead of the abandoned `agent:main:A`.
What was not tested: No live Gateway transport, embedded-backend runtime, provider, or session persistence was exercised; per `src/tui/CLAUDE.md` the backend RPC was driven through the injected `client.loadHistory` seam rather than a real socket. Full `pnpm build` was not run, since no module-boundary or packaging surface changed.

### Supplemental checks (head 66c6503daa, base 69554995c1)
- `node scripts/test-projects.mjs src/tui/tui-session-actions.test.ts` -> 40 passed, including the two race regressions (`keeps the newer session when an earlier switch's history resolves last`, `ignores a superseded switch whose history request rejects`).
- `node scripts/test-projects.mjs src/tui/tui-pty-harness.e2e.test.ts` -> 20 passed. This lane runs the real `runTui()` render loop over a fake `TuiBackend`, including the new `keeps the newer session when a rapid switch's history resolves last` case.
- `oxfmt --check`, `oxlint`, `tsgo` core and core-test typechecks, and the changed-file TypeScript LOC ratchet all pass on the four changed files.

### Note on the rebase
This branch was rebased onto current `main`. `main` has since gained a changed-file TypeScript LOC ratchet that forbids growth of production files already above 500 lines, and `src/tui/tui-session-actions.ts` sits at 666 lines. To stay under that baseline while adding the generation guard, `SessionInfoDefaults`, `SessionInfoEntry`, and the inline history record shape (now `TuiHistoryRecord`) were hoisted into `src/tui/tui-types.ts`, next to the `SessionInfo` and `TuiHistoryLoadResult` types they extend. That move is type-only with no runtime behavior change, and it brings the file to 658 lines.
